Abstract:

In the early stages of federated learning (FL), clients were commonly assumed to perform an identical number of local updates per communication round, leading researchers to adopt a simple weighted aggregation method (e.g., FedAvg) across clients' local models.
However, in real-world edge computing or Internet of Things (IoT) scenarios, device heterogeneity and data heterogeneity often cause clients to perform varying numbers of local updates.

To quantify the impact of clients' varying local computational efforts, one existing solution is FedNova, which leverages normalized aggregation to mitigate performance degradation caused by heterogeneous local updates. 
Building on FedNova, we establish a generalized convergence characterization that explicitly incorporates both data heterogeneity and the variable number of local updates, providing an analytical foundation for the server’s objective. 
Furthermore, by considering clients' two-dimensional private information—specifically computational cost and data heterogeneity—we explore a contract-based incentive design approach. 
We develop optimal contracts under three distinct information scenarios: complete, weakly incomplete, and strongly incomplete information.

Under complete and weakly incomplete information, we derive a closed-form solution showing that the server should only incentivize clients with both the lowest unit cost per local update and the lowest data heterogeneity.
Under strongly incomplete information, we transform the combinatorial problem into a convex optimization problem via a practical assumption of positive probabilities for client types.
Our method achieves average cost reductions of 15\% and 5.5\% over the uniform contract (UC) and Stackelberg game (SG) benchmarks, respectively.
